\section{Pass Tail Context Through a Core Syntax Tree}

A compiler can determine syntactic tail position before it knows any call targets. Apply the following walk to an expanded core with atoms, lambdas, calls, conditionals, sequences, and single-binding \texttt{let}. The input is a node and a flag saying whether its result is the result of the current lambda. The output is an annotation on each application: a marked call can use the current continuation unchanged, while an unmarked application must return to the surrounding evaluation context. This pass identifies syntactic tail calls only; the special invocation obligations of primitives such as \texttt{apply} belong in their implementations.

\begin{verbatim}
mark(expr, tail):
  case expr of
    lambda(body):
      # A lambda starts a fresh return context.
      mark(body, true)
    if(test, yes, no):
      mark(test, false)
      mark(yes, tail); mark(no, tail)
    let(rhs, body):
      mark(rhs, false); mark(body, tail)
    begin(prefix, last):
      for e in prefix: mark(e, false)
      mark(last, tail)
    call(op, args):
      # Computing a callee is not calling it.
      expr.tail = tail
      mark(op, false)
      for a in args: mark(a, false)
    atom: pass
\end{verbatim}

Trace a \texttt{let} binding \(x\) to the result of \texttt{read}. Its body tests \texttt{ok?(x)}. On success it calls \texttt{step} on the result of \texttt{next(x)}; on failure it calls \texttt{done(x)}. The walk marks only the calls to \texttt{step} and \texttt{done}. Suppose \texttt{read} returns \(6\), \texttt{ok?} returns true, \texttt{next} returns \(7\), and \texttt{step} doubles its argument. The calls are \texttt{read()}, \texttt{ok?(6)}, \texttt{next(6)}, and \texttt{step(7)}, in that order; the body returns \(14\). The temporary continuation for \texttt{next} still owes the application of \texttt{step}; treating both calls as tail transfers would return \(7\) instead.

The induction invariant is that a true flag reaches a node exactly when the surrounding core construct has no work after that node produces its result. A nested lambda resets the flag even if the lambda expression is, for example, an argument to another call. The pass visits each node once and needs traversal space proportional to syntax depth. It neither allocates continuation objects nor proves a bound on the space consumed by data.
